function data(input : dval) dval {
    var slug := string(input.slug, "")
    if slug == "collections-and-records" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-07-collections-and-records"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "collections-and-records"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/collections-and-records/"
        page["title"] = "7. Arrays, tuples, and structs"
        page["content"] = "Use an array for an ordered collection of one type. Use a tuple for a short fixed group. Use a struct when fields need names.\n\n## Arrays\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nAda\n\n```\n\nArray indexes start at zero. An invalid index traps.\n\nDeclare the item type when an array starts empty:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(\"Ada\")\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`push`, `pop`, `insert`, `remove`, `clear`, `reserve`, and `resize` change an array.\n\n## Array values\n\nAn array is a mutable reference value inside one workspace:\n\n```capy\nfunction append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], \" \", second[2], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAssignment and parameter passing share the array identity. Returns and captures also share it. Content mutation through a parameter affects the caller. A parameter binding remains immutable, so `values = [3]` is invalid. Array growth preserves identity.\n\nA `for` loop reads array items in order:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe optional index follows the value and has type `s32`. The loop rechecks the current array length. It safely stops after shrinkage and can visit appended items. The loop retains each current managed item while the body runs.\n\nThe loop item is a value, not an assignable array position. Use an index to replace an item.\n\n## Tuples\n\nA tuple is immutable. It has fixed positions and can contain different types:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(re"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "quest : dval) {\n    var result := (200, \"ok\")\n    print(result[0], \" \", result[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`(value)` only groups an expression. It does not create a one-item tuple.\n\nA tuple can spread into matching function parameters:\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -> left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Structs\n\nA struct defines a named fixed layout:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe generated constructor takes fields in declaration order. A struct instance is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity. Struct fields are assignable:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe field mutation affects the caller. The parameter binding itself remains immutable.\n\nA receiver-first function can use method syntax:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -> point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis is the same call as `total(point)`.\n\n## Choose the right shape\n\nUse an array when every item has one static type. Use a tuple for a small fixed return value. Use a struct for application data with a stable shape. Use a `dval` map or list for decoded input and data that crosses a Bearer boundary.\n"
        page["label"] = "7. Arrays, tuples, and structs"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["capy-03-values-and-types", "capy-05-functions-and-closures", "capy-08-dynamic-values", "length", "dval_put"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Ada\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names : [string] = []\n    names.push(\"Ada\")\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function append_three(values : [s32]) {\n    values.push(3)\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var first := [1, 2]\n    var second := first\n    append_three(second)\n    print(first[2], \" \", second[2], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    for name, index := names {\n        print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := (200, \"ok\")\n    print(result[0], \" \", result[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 { -> left + right }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var pair := (20, 22)\n    var answer := add(...pair)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction move_right(point : Point) {\n    point.x = point.x + 1\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    move_right(point)\n    print(point.x, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction total(point : Point) s32 { -> point.x + point.y }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.total(), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "dynamic-values" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-08-dynamic-values"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "dynamic-values"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/dynamic-values/"
        page["title"] = "8. Dynamic values"
        page["content"] = "A `dval` holds data whose shape is known only at run time. JSON, YAML, request data, task props, and unit calls use `dval` values.\n\n## Read decoded data\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := json_decode(\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ada\\\"}\")\n    print(profile.name, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nAda\n\n```\n\n`json_decode` returns a `dval`. Member syntax reads a map key. Bracket syntax also works:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    print(profile[\"name\"], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse brackets for a dynamic key, a key that is not an identifier, or a list index.\n\n## Missing data\n\nA missing read returns `none` instead of changing the map or trapping:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var nickname := profile.nickname\n    if nickname? {\n        print(nickname)\n    } else {\n        print(\"guest\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`value?` is false only when the `dval` is `none`. An empty string, `false`, an empty map, and an empty list are present values.\n\nA key can exist and contain `none`. Use `dval_has(profile, \"nickname\")` when that distinction matters.\n\n## Convert a scalar\n\nScalar constructors convert a `dval` to a static Capy type:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var name := string(profile.name)\n    var visits := s32(profile.visits, 0)\n    print(name, \" \", visits, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe second argument is a fallback. Capy uses it when the value is `none` or cannot convert to the requested type.\n\nUse `dval_require(profile, \"name\")` when a missing value is an error. It traps instead of returning `none`.\n\n## Create dynamic data\n\nA map literal creates a `dval` map:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var user := {name: \"Ada\", active: true}\n    var empty "
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + ":= {:}\n    print(user.name, \" \", empty?, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA bare list is a typed array. Wrap it with `dval` when it must be a dynamic list. `length(value)` returns the number of entries in a `dval` map or list. It traps if the `dval` is a scalar:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var tags := dval([\"math\", \"logic\"])\n    print(tags[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Mutate a dval\n\nA `dval` is a mutable reference value inside one workspace. Assignment, parameters, returns, and captures share its identity:\n\n```capy\nfunction set_email(profile : dval) {\n    profile.contact.email = \"ada@example.test\"\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {:}\n    var alias := profile\n    set_email(alias)\n    print(profile.contact.email, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nContent mutation through the parameter affects the caller. The parameter binding remains immutable, so `profile = {:}` is invalid.\n\nNested assignment mutates its target and returns the same identity. The root must be a named local, parameter, or captured `dval` binding. Temporary, call, member, array-item, and loop-item roots are invalid. Nested assignment creates missing maps. An existing list accepts only an in-range nonnegative index. Capy does not create sparse lists.\n\nThe `dval_set`, `dval_assign`, `dval_push`, `dval_pop`, `dval_remove`, `dval_clear`, `dval_get_or_create`, `dval_set_array`, `dval_set_bool`, `dval_set_type`, and `dval_put` functions also mutate their target. Each function returns that same identity. Reassignment remains valid:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {:}\n    profile = dval_put(profile, \"active\", true)\n    print(profile.active, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nMember and index reads return copied child values. Pure transformations return copies when their API specifies a copy.\n\n## Iterate a d"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "val\n\nUse the value-first loop forms:\n\n```capy\nfunction show(values : dval) {\n    for item := values { print(item, \"\\n\") }\n    for item, key := values { print(key, \": \", item, \"\\n\") }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show({name: \"Ada\"})\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe key has type `string`. `dval` list keys are decimal strings. Each item is a copied `dval`. The loop uses a live view and rechecks the current count. A loop that keeps growing its iterable can continue.\n\n## Boundaries\n\nA `dval` keeps identity only inside one workspace. Bearer, module, component, task, custom export, codec, request, and serialization boundaries copy BRRB. Identity never crosses a boundary. JSON and YAML preserve `none` as `null`. XML writes `none` as empty text and cannot recover the distinction.\n"
        page["label"] = "8. Dynamic values"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["0_DValue", "json_decode", "yaml_decode", "capy-07-collections-and-records", "capy-09-web-handlers-and-requests", "dval", "dval_put", "bool", "s32", "string", "length"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := json_decode(\"{\\\"name\\\":\\\"Ada\\\"}\")\n    print(profile.name, \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Ada\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    print(profile[\"name\"], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var nickname := profile.nickname\n    if nickname? {\n        print(nickname)\n    } else {\n        print(\"guest\")\n    }\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var name := string(profile.name)\n    var visits := s32(profile.visits, 0)\n    print(name, \" \", visits, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var user := {name: \"Ada\", active: true}\n    var empty := {:}\n    print(user.name, \" \", empty?, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var tags := dval([\"math\", \"logic\"])\n    print(tags[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function set_email(profile : dval) {\n    profile.contact.email = \"ada@example.test\"\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {:}\n    var alias := profile\n    set_email(alias)\n    print(profile.contact.email, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {:}\n    profile = dval_put(profile, \"active\", true)\n    print(profile.active, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function show(values : dval) {\n    for item := values { print(item, \"\\n\") }\n    for item, key := values { print(key, \": \", item, \"\\n\") }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show({name: \"Ada\"})\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "errors-testing-and-style" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-12-errors-testing-and-style"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "errors-testing-and-style"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/errors-testing-and-style/"
        page["title"] = "12. Errors and testing"
        page["content"] = "Capy reports type and syntax errors before a unit runs. Runtime failures include the original Capy source location.\n\n## Handle expected missing data\n\nUse a fallback when absence is valid:\n\n```capy\nfunction display_name(profile : dval) string {\n    -> string(profile.name, \"guest\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(display_name({:}), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nguest\n\n```\n\nThe fallback handles both `none` and failed string conversion. It does not hide a compiler error or an unrelated runtime trap.\n\n## Compiler errors\n\nThe compiler rejects invalid syntax, unknown names, and incompatible types. For example, this assignment is invalid:\n\n```capy-error\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var count := 1\n    count = \"one\"\n}\n```\n\n`count` has type `s32`. Assignment cannot change its type to `string`.\n\nRead the first diagnostic and its source location. Later messages can be consequences of the first error.\n\n## Runtime traps\n\nUse strict access when missing data violates the program contract:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {:}\n    var name := dval_require(profile, \"name\")\n    print(name)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis traps if `name` is missing or the access path is unusable. Other traps include division by zero, invalid typed-array indexes, failed host operations, and an explicit `trap()`.\n\nDo not use a fallback for data that the program requires. A clear trap at the boundary is safer than incorrect work with a fabricated value.\n\n## Test through Bearer\n\nRun one page through the local CLI socket:\n\n```text\nscripts/bearer-cli /path/to/page.capy\n```\n\nThe CLI helper exposes the response body and runtime errors. Use an HTTP client or a repository HTTP test helper when a test must verify status and headers. Include failure cases for missing input, invalid"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + " conversions, and boundary errors.\n\nThe repository test suites exercise the compiler, Wasm runtime, source maps, components, units, tasks, and documentation examples:\n\n```text\nscripts/test_capy_native.sh\nscripts/test_capy_phase1.sh\nscripts/test_capy_reference.sh\n```\n\nRun the focused test while editing. Run the broader gate before deployment.\n\n## Keep code direct\n\nPrefer a small typed helper over repeated dynamic conversion. Keep request parsing near the request boundary. Convert a `dval` to a static type once the required shape is known.\n\nUse one name for one thing. Remove wrappers that only rename another function. Keep comments for behavior that the code cannot make clear.\n\n## Find API details\n\nThe language guide explains how Capy code fits together. The API reference gives exact standard-library signatures and failure behavior. Use the generated Capy signature on each API page.\n\n## Read failures as contracts\n\nA compile error usually means the source does not match the type contract. Fix that error before you inspect later messages.\n\nA runtime trap usually means the input did not match the application contract. Use `dval_require()` when the contract requires data. Use constructors with fallbacks when absence is valid.\n\nWrite tests for both paths. A success test proves the expected shape works. A failure test proves bad input cannot silently continue.\n"
        page["label"] = "12. Errors and testing"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["capy-08-dynamic-values", "capy-09-web-handlers-and-requests", "trap"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function display_name(profile : dval) string {\n    -> string(profile.name, \"guest\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(display_name({:}), \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "guest\n"}, {entry: "cli", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {:}\n    var name := dval_require(profile, \"name\")\n    print(name)\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "expressions-and-control-flow" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-04-expressions-and-control-flow"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "expressions-and-control-flow"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/expressions-and-control-flow/"
        page["title"] = "4. Expressions and control flow"
        page["content"] = "An expression produces a value or performs an action. Capy evaluates listed expressions from left to right.\n\n## Calculate a value\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var price := 6\n    var quantity := 2\n    var total := price * quantity\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n12\n\n```\n\n`*` runs before `+` and `-`. Parentheses make the intended order explicit.\n\n## Declare and assign locals\n\n`:=` declares a local and infers its type. `=` replaces its value:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var count := 1\n    count = count + 1\n    print(count, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA local exists until the end of its block. An inner block can declare a new local with the same name without changing the outer local.\n\n## Conditions\n\nUse `if` to select code:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := 12\n    if total > 10 {\n        print(\"large\\n\")\n    } else {\n        print(\"small\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe condition must have type `bool`. `&&` and `||` stop as soon as the result is known.\n\nAn `if` with `else` can also produce a value:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := 12\n    var label := if total > 10 {\n        -> \"large\"\n    } else {\n        -> \"small\"\n    }\n    print(label, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nEach reachable branch that produces a value must produce the same type. A branch that returns or traps does not produce a value.\n\n## Loops\n\nA `while` loop repeats while its condition is true:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var index := 0\n    while index < 3 {\n        print(index, \"\\n\")\n        index = index + 1\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA `for` loop reads a half-open `s32` range, an array, or a `dval` map or list:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    for number := 1..4 {\n        print(number, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe range is"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + " half-open. `1..4` contains `1`, `2`, and `3`. A range permits one loop value only.\n\nOptional metadata follows the value:\n\n```capy\nfunction show(names : [string], profile : dval) {\n    for name, index := names { print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\") }\n    for item, key := profile { print(key, \": \", item, \"\\n\") }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show([\"Ada\"], {role: \"admin\"})\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAn array index has type `s32`. A `dval` key has type `string`. `dval` list keys are decimal strings. Structs are not iterable. The old `=` and `in` forms are invalid.\n\nArray and `dval` loops recheck the current count before each iteration. A loop can visit items added while it runs. Shrinkage cannot cause an invalid access. `dval` loop items are copies. An array loop retains its current managed item while the body runs.\n\nUse `break` to leave the nearest loop. Use `continue` to start its next iteration.\n\n## Blocks\n\nA block yields a value when its final reachable item starts with `->`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := {\n        var subtotal := 10\n        -> subtotal + 2\n    }\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThis makes `total` equal to `12`. A block can keep temporary locals close to the calculation that uses them.\n"
        page["label"] = "4. Expressions and control flow"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["capy-03-values-and-types", "capy-05-functions-and-closures", "capy-07-collections-and-records"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var price := 6\n    var quantity := 2\n    var total := price * quantity\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "12\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var count := 1\n    count = count + 1\n    print(count, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := 12\n    if total > 10 {\n        print(\"large\\n\")\n    } else {\n        print(\"small\\n\")\n    }\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := 12\n    var label := if total > 10 {\n        -> \"large\"\n    } else {\n        -> \"small\"\n    }\n    print(label, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var index := 0\n    while index < 3 {\n        print(index, \"\\n\")\n        index = index + 1\n    }\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    for number := 1..4 {\n        print(number, \"\\n\")\n    }\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function show(names : [string], profile : dval) {\n    for name, index := names { print(index, \": \", name, \"\\n\") }\n    for item, key := profile { print(key, \": \", item, \"\\n\") }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show([\"Ada\"], {role: \"admin\"})\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := {\n        var subtotal := 10\n        -> subtotal + 2\n    }\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "functions-and-closures" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-05-functions-and-closures"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "functions-and-closures"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/functions-and-closures/"
        page["title"] = "5. Functions"
        page["content"] = "A function gives a name to a calculation. Parameters describe its input. The declared result type describes its output.\n\n## Define and call a function\n\n```capy\nfunction greet(name : string) string {\n    -> \"Hello, \" + name\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(greet(\"Ada\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, Ada\n\n```\n\nThe `->` expression supplies the function result. Use `return` when a function must finish early.\n\n## Parameters and defaults\n\nParameters have names and types:\n\n```capy\nfunction label(name : string, suffix : string = \"!\") string {\n    -> name + suffix\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(label(\"Capy\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`label(\"Capy\")` uses the default suffix. Defaults are trailing literals. A direct call can omit them, but a function value keeps the full parameter list.\n\nCapy evaluates arguments once from left to right.\n\n## Overloads\n\nSeveral functions can have the same name when their parameter types differ:\n\n```capy\nfunction describe(value : s32) string { -> \"integer\" }\nfunction describe(value : string) string { -> \"string\" }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(describe(42), \" \", describe(\"Ada\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe argument types select the overload. A result type never selects one.\n\n## Conversion parameters\n\nA parameter declared with `as` permits one constructor conversion:\n\n```capy\nfunction show(value : as string) {\n    print(value, \"\\n\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show(42)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe call behaves as if it passed `string(42)`. Capy does not chain several conversions.\n\n## Function values\n\nA function can be stored and called through a typed value:\n\n```capy\nfunction double(value : s32) s32 { -> value * 2 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var operation : function(value : s32) s32 = double\n    var answe"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "r := operation(21)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA lambda defines a small function at its point of use:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var add_one := function(value : s32) s32 {\n        -> value + 1\n    }\n    print(add_one(41), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA lambda can read captured outer values. Scalar captures copy. Array, `dval`, and struct captures share identity inside the workspace. A capture binding cannot be reassigned. The closure can mutate shared aggregate content.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var offset := 2\n    var values := [10]\n    var update := function() s32 {\n        values.push(20)\n        -> offset + length(values)\n    }\n    print(update(), \" \", values[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Generic and variadic functions\n\nAn `any` parameter creates a compile-time generic function. Use it when the same operation is valid for several concrete types.\n\nA variadic parameter collects values into a typed array:\n\n```capy\nfunction print_all(...values : as string) {\n    for value := values {\n        print(value)\n    }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print_all(\"one\", \" \", \"two\", \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `...items` to spread an array into a variadic call.\n"
        page["label"] = "5. Functions"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["capy-04-expressions-and-control-flow", "capy-07-collections-and-records", "print"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function greet(name : string) string {\n    -> \"Hello, \" + name\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(greet(\"Ada\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Hello, Ada\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function label(name : string, suffix : string = \"!\") string {\n    -> name + suffix\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(label(\"Capy\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function describe(value : s32) string { -> \"integer\" }\nfunction describe(value : string) string { -> \"string\" }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(describe(42), \" \", describe(\"Ada\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function show(value : as string) {\n    print(value, \"\\n\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    show(42)\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function double(value : s32) s32 { -> value * 2 }\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var operation : function(value : s32) s32 = double\n    var answer := operation(21)\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var add_one := function(value : s32) s32 {\n        -> value + 1\n    }\n    print(add_one(41), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var offset := 2\n    var values := [10]\n    var update := function() s32 {\n        values.push(20)\n        -> offset + length(values)\n    }\n    print(update(), \" \", values[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function print_all(...values : as string) {\n    for value := values {\n        print(value)\n    }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print_all(\"one\", \" \", \"two\", \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "install-and-first-program" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-01-install-and-first-program"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "install-and-first-program"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/install-and-first-program/"
        page["title"] = "1. Getting started"
        page["content"] = "Capy programs run as Bearer units. A source file uses the `.capy` suffix. Bearer compiles the file when a request first uses it.\n\n## Your first page\n\nCreate `site/hello.capy`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"Hello, Capy!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, Capy!\n\n```\n\n`RENDER` is the entry point for a web request. `print` adds text to the response. The `\\n` escape adds a newline.\n\nRequest the page through Bearer's default local HTTP socket:\n\n```text\ncurl --unix-socket /run/bearer/http.sock http://localhost/hello.capy\n```\n\nThe command prints the response body. Bearer logs compiler and runtime errors.\n\n## Add a value\n\nA local declaration uses `:=`. Capy infers the type from the value:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    print(\"Hello, \", name, \"!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`print` accepts several arguments and writes them in order.\n\n## Write HTML\n\nA markup expression produces escaped HTML:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    print(<><h1>Hello, <?= name ?>!</h1></>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`print` writes the markup value to the response. A markup expression alone does not produce output. The markup chapter explains escaping and trusted markup.\n\n## If it does not run\n\nConfirm that `bearer.service` is active. The source file must be below the configured site directory. The CLI helper must use the configured local socket.\n\n## Make the first change small\n\nChange one value and request the page again. For example, change `Ada` to your name. This proves that Bearer sees the file you edited.\n\nThen add one standard-library call, such as `response_header(\"Content-Type\", \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\")`. Small changes make compiler errors easier to isolate.\n\nContinue with basic source syntax before you add request data or appl"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "ication APIs.\n"
        page["label"] = "1. Getting started"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["1_RENDER", "1_CLI", "print", "capy-02-source-structure-and-syntax", "capy-06-strings-and-markup"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"Hello, Capy!\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Hello, Capy!\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    print(\"Hello, \", name, \"!\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    print(<><h1>Hello, <?= name ?>!</h1></>)\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "source-structure-and-syntax" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-02-source-structure-and-syntax"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "source-structure-and-syntax"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/source-structure-and-syntax/"
        page["title"] = "2. Basic syntax"
        page["content"] = "A Capy file contains declarations. Functions, handlers, structs, and type aliases are declarations. Code that performs work belongs inside a function or handler.\n\n## A complete source file\n\n```capy\n// A comment continues to the end of the line.\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var greeting := \"Hello\"\n    print(greeting, \", Capy!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, Capy!\n\n```\n\nThis file has one comment and one handler. Names are case-sensitive, so `greeting` and `Greeting` are different names.\n\n## Functions\n\nA function declaration has a name, parameters, a result type, and a body:\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 {\n    -> left + right\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(add(20, 22), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe `->` expression supplies the function result. A function that returns no value has the result type `void`. Handler declarations omit a result type because handlers write a response instead of returning one.\n\n## Statements and expressions\n\nWhitespace separates tokens but does not end an expression. Commas separate arguments and collection items. Most lines do not need a semicolon.\n\n```capy\nfunction add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 {\n    -> left + right\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := add(\n        20,\n        22\n    )\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA block starts with `{` and ends with `}`. Its expressions run from top to bottom.\n\n## Comments\n\nUse `//` for a line comment:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    // Explain why the next operation is necessary.\n    var retries := 3\n    print(retries, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nComments do not change the program. Prefer names that make routine code clear without a comment.\n\n## Reserved names\n\nApplication code cannot declare host functions. Names that start with `__bearer_` ar"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "e reserved for the standard library. Later chapters introduce the handler names that Bearer calls.\n\n## Keep the unit shape simple\n\nPut helper functions near the handler that uses them. Move a helper only when several units share it. This keeps the request flow easy to read.\n\nUse one top-level entry point for each route. Add named handlers only when the route needs separate component, WebSocket, task, or export entry points.\n\nA source file can declare types, structs, functions, and handlers in any order. Prefer the order that helps the next reader: data shapes first, helpers next, and handlers last.\n\n## Read syntax errors locally\n\nThe compiler reports the source path, line, and column. Look at that location first. If a block misses `-> expression`, the error points at the value-producing block.\n\nDo not use a newline to end an expression. Newlines are whitespace in normal code. Use braces and explicit block yields to show where a value comes from.\n"
        page["label"] = "2. Basic syntax"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["capy-03-values-and-types", "capy-04-expressions-and-control-flow", "1_RENDER", "1_COMPONENT"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "// A comment continues to the end of the line.\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var greeting := \"Hello\"\n    print(greeting, \", Capy!\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Hello, Capy!\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 {\n    -> left + right\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(add(20, 22), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function add(left : s32, right : s32) s32 {\n    -> left + right\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total := add(\n        20,\n        22\n    )\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    // Explain why the next operation is necessary.\n    var retries := 3\n    print(retries, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "strings-and-markup" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-06-strings-and-markup"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "strings-and-markup"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/strings-and-markup/"
        page["title"] = "6. Strings and HTML"
        page["content"] = "Use strings for text and markup expressions for HTML. Markup escapes ordinary strings before they enter the response.\n\n## Escape dynamic text\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"<Ada>\"\n    print(<><p>Hello, <?= name ?></p></>, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n<p>Hello, &lt;Ada&gt;</p>\n\n```\n\n`<?= name ?>` selects an encoding from its location. HTML text and quoted attributes escape `&`, `<`, `>`, quotes, and apostrophes.\n\nInside a `script` element, a string becomes one self-contained JavaScript literal. The encoder protects the closing tag and JavaScript line separators. Inside a `style` element, a string becomes one self-contained CSS literal. Integer and Boolean values remain scalar values. `f64` interpolation is not supported in these elements.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, \"guest\")\n    print(<>\n        <script>const name = <?= name ?>;</script>\n        <style>.name::before { content: <?= name ?>; }</style>\n    </>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nScript and style interpolation must start at a value boundary. Static source after it can apply an operator or a CSS unit. Do not put interpolation inside an existing string, template literal, or comment. Attribute values must use quotes. Tag and attribute names cannot contain interpolation.\n\nURL, event-handler, and inline-style attributes use HTML attribute escaping. Validate these values for their application context before interpolation.\n\n## String literals\n\nString literals use double quotes:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var message := \"Hello\"\n    var line := \"first\\nsecond\"\n    print(message, \" \", line, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe supported escapes include `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, `\\\"`, and `\\\\`. String positions and lengths count bytes, not Unicode characters.\n"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "\nUse `+` to join strings:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    var greeting := \"Hello, \" + name\n    print(greeting, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse a constructor to convert a scalar when a function does not already accept `as string`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var label := \"Count: \" + string(3)\n    print(label, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Markup expressions\n\nA markup expression starts with `<>` and ends with `</>`:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var title := \"Report\"\n    print(<>\n        <h1><?= title ?></h1>\n        <p>Ready</p>\n    </>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe expression has type `markup`. It is a value. It does not write to the response by itself. Use `print(markup_value)` to output it. You can also return `markup` from an ordinary function or insert it into other markup.\n\n```capy\nfunction badge(label : string) markup {\n    -> <><strong><?= label ?></strong></>\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(badge(\"Ready\"))\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n<strong>Ready</strong>\n\n```\n\n\n## Trusted markup\n\nA `markup` value stays trusted when `<?= value ?>` inserts it into HTML text. In attributes, scripts, and styles, Capy escapes it as a string for that context. `<?: value ?>` also inserts a `markup` value without escaping it again. Raw interpolation works only in HTML text:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var icon := trusted_markup(\"<strong>ready</strong>\")\n    print(<><p><?: icon ?></p></>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `trusted_markup` only at a reviewed trust boundary. Do not apply it to request input, database text, or remote content.\n\n## Common string operations\n\nUse `length`, `substr`, `find`, `contains`, `replace`, `lower`, `upper`, `split`, and `join` for ordinary text work. Use `html_escape` when an API needs escaped text instead o"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "f a `markup` value.\n\nStrings use automatic reference counting. Assignment and function calls preserve their lifetime. Most programs do not need to call `clone`.\n"
        page["label"] = "6. Strings and HTML"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["0_String", "html_escape", "capy-05-functions-and-closures", "capy-09-web-handlers-and-requests", "string", "length", "trusted_markup", "clone"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"<Ada>\"\n    print(<><p>Hello, <?= name ?></p></>, \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "<p>Hello, &lt;Ada&gt;</p>\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, \"guest\")\n    print(<>\n        <script>const name = <?= name ?>;</script>\n        <style>.name::before { content: <?= name ?>; }</style>\n    </>)\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var message := \"Hello\"\n    var line := \"first\\nsecond\"\n    print(message, \" \", line, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    var greeting := \"Hello, \" + name\n    print(greeting, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var label := \"Count: \" + string(3)\n    print(label, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var title := \"Report\"\n    print(<>\n        <h1><?= title ?></h1>\n        <p>Ready</p>\n    </>)\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function badge(label : string) markup {\n    -> <><strong><?= label ?></strong></>\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(badge(\"Ready\"))\n}\n", output: "<strong>Ready</strong>\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var icon := trusted_markup(\"<strong>ready</strong>\")\n    print(<><p><?: icon ?></p></>)\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "tasks-and-jobs" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-11-tasks-and-jobs"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "tasks-and-jobs"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/tasks-and-jobs/"
        page["title"] = "11. Tasks and jobs"
        page["content"] = "A task runs a Capy handler outside the current request. A job runs an operating-system command. Both return immediately with a handle that the caller can inspect later.\n\n## Read status data\n\nTask status is a `dval` because fields can vary with the state:\n\n```capy\nfunction state_label(status : dval) string {\n    -> string(status.state, \"missing\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(state_label({state: \"queued\"}), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nqueued\n\n```\n\nReal task states are `queued`, `running`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `canceled`, or `missing`.\n\n## Define a task handler\n\nA named task handler receives one request value:\n\n```capy\nfunction TASK:STORE(request : dval) {\n    var props := request.props\n    var key := string(props.key, \"\")\n    if key == \"\" {\n        trap()\n    }\n    // Store the application result under key.\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"task handler ready\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe task boundary copies props as BRRB. `dval` identity does not cross it. The worker does not receive the caller's request, session, connections, open handles, or memory state.\n\n## Submit a task\n\n`task` returns an opaque string ID after Bearer admits the task:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var id := task(\"/tasks/store.capy:STORE\", {key: \"report-42\"})\n    print(id, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nThe call traps if Bearer cannot admit the task. Use `task_status(id)` to poll. Use `task_await(id, timeout_ms)` to wait for a state:\n\n```capy\nfunction show_failure(id : string) {\n    var status := task_await(id, 5000)\n    if string(status.state, \"\") == \"failed\" {\n        print(string(status.failure_code, \"unknown\"))\n    }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"failure helper ready\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA wait timeout does not cancel active work. `task_cancel(id)` requests "
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "cancellation, but a running task can finish first. Cancellation does not undo an external effect.\n\nA successful task means that its handler finished. Task status does not carry an application result. Store results in application-owned persistent storage.\n\n## Run a command\n\n`shell_spawn` starts a bounded operating-system job:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var job := shell_spawn({cmd: \"printf ready\", timeout_ms: 1000})\n    var status := job_await(job, 3000)\n    print(string(status.state, \"missing\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `job_status`, `job_await`, `job_result`, and `job_cancel` with the returned handle. Always set a deliberate command timeout. Validate the final state before reading result data.\n\nThe shell interprets `cmd`. Do not build it from request data or other untrusted input.\n\n## Failure and retries\n\nBearer does not retry a failed task automatically. Submit a new task only when the application can repeat the operation safely. Use an application idempotency key for work that changes external state.\n\nDo not wait for long work in a page request unless the response requires it. Treat task IDs and job handles as opaque values.\n\n## Keep task input small\n\nPass a `dval` that contains only stable identifiers and simple options. Do not pass a full request snapshot to a task. Request data can contain cookies, headers, and uploaded-file metadata that the task does not need.\n\nUse a database row, file path, or application key when work needs large input. Validate that reference again inside the task. A task can run after the user request has ended.\n\nUse a visible status page for long work. It should show queued, running, done, failed, and canceled states in user terms.\n"
        page["label"] = "11. Tasks and jobs"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["task", "task_status", "task_await", "task_cancel", "shell_spawn", "job_status", "job_result", "job_await", "job_cancel", "capy-08-dynamic-values", "capy-12-errors-testing-and-style"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function state_label(status : dval) string {\n    -> string(status.state, \"missing\")\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(state_label({state: \"queued\"}), \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "queued\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function TASK:STORE(request : dval) {\n    var props := request.props\n    var key := string(props.key, \"\")\n    if key == \"\" {\n        trap()\n    }\n    // Store the application result under key.\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"task handler ready\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "cli", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var id := task(\"/tasks/store.capy:STORE\", {key: \"report-42\"})\n    print(id, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function show_failure(id : string) {\n    var status := task_await(id, 5000)\n    if string(status.state, \"\") == \"failed\" {\n        print(string(status.failure_code, \"unknown\"))\n    }\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    print(\"failure helper ready\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var job := shell_spawn({cmd: \"printf ready\", timeout_ms: 1000})\n    var status := job_await(job, 3000)\n    print(string(status.state, \"missing\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "units-components-and-exports" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-10-units-components-and-exports"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "units-components-and-exports"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/units-components-and-exports/"
        page["title"] = "10. Units and components"
        page["content"] = "Each Capy source file is an independent unit. A unit can expose functions and type metadata to other units. It can also render named components.\n\n## Export a function\n\n`#exports` lists the functions and types that another unit can inspect. A callable `(dval) dval` function can also be called through a module handle:\n\n```capy\n#exports echo\n\nfunction echo(input : dval) dval {\n    -> input\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := echo({message: \"ready\"})\n    print(result.message, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nready\n\n```\n\nA module-callable function has the exact signature `(dval) dval`. Private helper functions do not appear in `#exports`.\n\n## Call another unit\n\nA separate service unit can expose a data function:\n\n```capy\n#exports version\n\nfunction version(input : dval) dval {\n    -> {name: \"Capy\"}\n}\n\n```\n\n\nLoad and call it from a neighboring page:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var service := unit_load(\"/doc/examples/call_target.capy\")\n    var version := service.default_input()\n    print(string(version), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`unit_load` returns a request-local `module` handle. Module member calls are dynamic. `service.default_input()` calls the exported function named `default_input` on that loaded unit. Module calls copy input and result `dval` values as BRRB. `unit_call(path, name, input)` performs the same operation without keeping a handle.\n\nRelative paths resolve from the calling unit. Use an absolute site path when the target must not depend on the caller directory.\n\n## Module handles\n\nA module handle is a verified request-local capability. It can pass through typed locals, parameters, and results during that request. It cannot enter a `dval`, aggregate, closure, condition, or serialized boundary.\n\n## Static type imports\n\nUse `#import \"pat"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "h\" as name` when the compiler must see exported Capy types from another unit. For example, `#import \"/doc/examples/call_target.capy\" as example` makes `example.VersionInfo` valid in type positions.\n\nA static import reads type metadata at compile time. It does not create a runtime module handle. Use `unit_load()` when the target unit must be selected at runtime.\n\nDo not retain a module handle for another request. A later request can load a newer valid source generation.\n\n## Components\n\nA component writes reusable response content through output functions such as `print`. A named component uses a handler name:\n\n```capy\nfunction COMPONENT:CARD(request : dval) {\n    var props := request.props\n    print(<><article><?= string(props.title, \"Untitled\") ?></article></>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\nRender it into the response:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    component_render(\"/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD\", {title: \"News\"})\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `component_capture` when the caller needs the component output as a string. Bearer copies props into the component request context and restores the earlier props after the call.\n\n## Boundary rules\n\nBearer copies `dval` values at module, component, task, export, codec, request, and serialization boundaries. A target cannot mutate the caller's value through a boundary. The dynamic-values guide defines the identity rule.\n\nMissing units, missing exports, and incompatible exported signatures are errors.\n\n## Choose a boundary\n\nUse a component when the result is markup for the current page. Use an exported function when the result is data. Use a direct helper function only inside one source file.\n\nA module loaded with `unit_load()` lives only for the current request. Store data in a session, a file, a database, or a task result when it must outlive the reques"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "t.\n\nKeep component props narrow. Pass only the values the component needs. This makes examples, tests, and future replacement easier.\n"
        page["label"] = "10. Units and components"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["unit_load", "unit_call", "component_render", "1_COMPONENT", "capy-08-dynamic-values", "capy-11-tasks-and-jobs", "component_capture", "call", "dval"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "#exports echo\n\nfunction echo(input : dval) dval {\n    -> input\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var result := echo({message: \"ready\"})\n    print(result.message, \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "ready\n"}, {entry: "cli", caption: "", body: "#exports version\n\nfunction version(input : dval) dval {\n    -> {name: \"Capy\"}\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var service := unit_load(\"/doc/examples/call_target.capy\")\n    var version := service.default_input()\n    print(string(version), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "cli", caption: "", body: "function COMPONENT:CARD(request : dval) {\n    var props := request.props\n    print(<><article><?= string(props.title, \"Untitled\") ?></article></>)\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    component_render(\"/doc/examples/card.capy:CARD\", {title: \"News\"})\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "values-and-types" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-03-values-and-types"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "values-and-types"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/values-and-types/"
        page["title"] = "3. Types"
        page["content"] = "Every Capy value has a type. The compiler checks types before the program runs. It usually infers the type of a local value.\n\n## Integers\n\nAn integer literal has type `s32` when no other type is stated:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var answer := 42\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\n42\n\n```\n\nState a wider type on the local, or construct the value explicitly:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var signed : s64 = 8\n    var inferred_signed := s64(9)\n    var unsigned : u64 = 10\n    var inferred_unsigned := u64(11)\n    print(signed, \" \", inferred_signed, \" \", unsigned, \" \", inferred_unsigned, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nBoth forms produce the stated wide integer type. `var signed : s64 = s64(8)` also compiles, but the constructor repeats information already supplied by the annotation.\n\nParameters, function results, assignments, typed arrays, struct fields, and the other operand in an expression can also state the integer type. Thus, a function that accepts `u64` can receive the plain literal `8`. An unconstrained literal remains `s32`.\n\nArithmetic requires operands of the same type. Use a constructor when the surrounding code does not state the required type.\n\n## Booleans and floating-point numbers\n\nA `bool` is either `true` or `false`. Conditions require a boolean value.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var enabled := true\n    var ratio := 2.5\n    print(enabled, \" \", ratio, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA decimal point or exponent makes an `f64` literal.\n\n## Strings\n\nA `string` stores bytes:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    var empty := \"\"\n    print(name, empty, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAn empty string is still a string value. It is not missing data.\n\n## Arrays, tuples, and structs\n\nAn array contains values of one t"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "ype:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA tuple has a fixed number of positions, which can have different types:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := (3, 4)\n    print(point[0], \" \", point[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nA struct gives names to fixed fields:\n\n```capy\nstruct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nArrays and struct instances are mutable reference values inside one workspace. Assignment shares their identity. Tuples remain immutable.\n\nThe collections chapter covers these types in detail.\n\n## Dynamic values and none\n\nA `dval` holds data whose shape is known only at run time. The `none` value represents missing dynamic data:\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var missing := none\n    print(profile.name, \" \", missing?, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`none` is different from `\"\"`, `false`, an empty map, and an empty list. A `dval` is also a mutable reference value inside one workspace. The dynamic-values chapter explains mutation, safe reads, and conversions.\n\n## Type aliases\n\nA type alias gives another name to a type:\n\n```capy\ntype Count = s64\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total : Count = 12\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\nAn alias does not create a new runtime type.\n"
        page["label"] = "3. Types"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["capy-04-expressions-and-control-flow", "capy-07-collections-and-records", "capy-08-dynamic-values", "0_DValue", "bool", "s32", "s64", "u64", "f64", "string", "dval", "length"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var answer := 42\n    print(answer, \"\\n\")\n}\n", output: "42\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var signed : s64 = 8\n    var inferred_signed := s64(9)\n    var unsigned : u64 = 10\n    var inferred_unsigned := u64(11)\n    print(signed, \" \", inferred_signed, \" \", unsigned, \" \", inferred_unsigned, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var enabled := true\n    var ratio := 2.5\n    print(enabled, \" \", ratio, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := \"Ada\"\n    var empty := \"\"\n    print(name, empty, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var names := [\"Ada\", \"Grace\"]\n    print(names[0], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := (3, 4)\n    print(point[0], \" \", point[1], \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "struct Point {\n    x : s32\n    y : s32\n}\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var point := Point(3, 4)\n    print(point.x, \" \", point.y, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var profile := {name: \"Ada\"}\n    var missing := none\n    print(profile.name, \" \", missing?, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "type Count = s64\n\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var total : Count = 12\n    print(total, \"\\n\")\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    if slug == "web-handlers-and-requests" {
        var page := dval({source_page: ""})
        page["source_page"] = "capy-09-web-handlers-and-requests"
        page["kind"] = "guide"
        page["slug"] = "web-handlers-and-requests"
        page["route"] = "/doc/guide/web-handlers-and-requests/"
        page["title"] = "9. Handling a web request"
        page["content"] = "A Capy Bearer handler receives one copied request snapshot. Declare every handler with `request : dval`.\n\n## Read the request snapshot\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, \"guest\")\n    response_header(\"Content-Type\", \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\")\n    print(\"Hello, \", name, \"!\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n**Output**\n\n```text\nHello, guest!\n\n```\n\nThe snapshot contains `method`, `query`, `form`, `headers`, `cookies`, `body`, `files`, `route`, `url`, `server`, `session`, `props`, `config`, `call`, `connection`, `unit`, and `websocket`.\n\n`route` contains `path`, `page`, `raw_path`, and `valid`. `url` contains `script` and `base`. `session` contains `id`, `name`, and `values`. `websocket` contains `connection_id`, `scope`, `opcode`, `binary`, and current-scope `connections`.\n\nMissing members are `none`. Use normal conversions for scalar values. For example, use `string(request.form.email, \"\")`, `s32(request.websocket.opcode, 0)`, and `bool(request.websocket.binary, false)`.\n\n## Form a response\n\nUse `print`, `response_status`, `response_header`, `response_cookie`, and `redirect` to change the response. Set headers before large output. Use a redirect after a successful state change so the user can refresh the page safely.\n\nUse `ws_send`, `ws_send_to`, and `ws_close` for WebSocket effects. Use `request.websocket.connections` to select current-scope clients.\n\n## Handle a form\n\nA handler normally uses one route for both the form and the POST result. Read `request.method` first. Read submitted fields from `request.form`.\n\nThis example shows the complete control flow. A GET request prints the form. A POST request validates the email, stores it in the session, queues a cookie, and redirects. Use `print` for every markup value that the handler must send. "
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "The documentation renderer runs the GET path.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    response_header(\"Content-Type\", \"text/html; charset=utf-8\")\n    var method := string(request.method, \"GET\")\n\n    if method == \"POST\" {\n        var email := string(request.form.email, \"\")\n        if email == \"\" {\n            response_status(422, \"Unprocessable Content\")\n            print(<><p>Email is required.</p></>)\n            return\n        }\n\n        var session := session_start(\"account\")\n        session = session_set(\"email\", email)\n        response_cookie(\"has_account\", \"1\")\n        redirect(\"/account\")\n        return\n    }\n\n    print(<>\n        <form method=\"post\">\n            <label>Email <input name=\"email\" type=\"email\" /></label>\n            <button>Save</button>\n        </form>\n    </>)\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Read uploaded files\n\nUploaded files appear in `request.files`. Each entry is a copied `dval` with the metadata that Bearer accepted for the request. Treat the original filename as user input.\n\nUse the generated request page for the exact file fields. Validate the file count, size, and type before you store anything.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var files := request.files\n    print(\"files=\", length(files), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## Sessions\n\nSession effects return a state map with `id`, `name`, and `values`. Keep the returned value when later code needs changed session data. The handler request snapshot does not change.\n\n```capy\nfunction RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var session := session_start(\"account\")\n    session = session_set(\"theme\", \"dark\")\n    print(string(session.values.theme, \"\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n\n```\n\n\n## CLI and WebSocket handlers\n\nUse `CLI` for a local command through Bearer's private CLI socket. Read command input from the copied request snapshot:\n\n```capy\n"
        page["content"] = string(page["content"], "") + "function CLI(request : dval) {\n    print(string(request.body, \"\"))\n}\n\n```\n\n\nUse `WS` for each WebSocket message. Read the message from `request.body`. Read the connection ID, scope, opcode, binary flag, and current-scope connections from `request.websocket`:\n\n```capy\nfunction WS(request : dval) {\n    ws_send(string(request.body, \"\"), false)\n}\n\n```\n\n\n`RENDER`, named `RENDER`, `COMPONENT`, named `COMPONENT`, `CLI`, `WS`, `TASK`, named `TASK`, `INIT`, `ONCE`, and custom HTTP handlers use `request : dval`. Handlers do not return application values.\n"
        page["label"] = "9. Handling a web request"
        page["capy_sig_lines"] = dval([])
        page["param_lines"] = dval([])
        page["notes"] = dval([])
        page["warnings"] = dval([])
        page["see"] = dval(["0_Request", "1_RENDER", "1_CLI", "session_start", "capy-08-dynamic-values", "capy-10-units-components-and-exports", "response_status", "response_header", "response_cookie", "session_set", "session_remove", "bool", "s32", "string"])
        page["examples"] = dval([])
        page["guide_examples"] = dval([{entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var name := string(request.query.name, \"guest\")\n    response_header(\"Content-Type\", \"text/plain; charset=utf-8\")\n    print(\"Hello, \", name, \"!\\n\")\n}\n", output: "Hello, guest!\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    response_header(\"Content-Type\", \"text/html; charset=utf-8\")\n    var method := string(request.method, \"GET\")\n\n    if method == \"POST\" {\n        var email := string(request.form.email, \"\")\n        if email == \"\" {\n            response_status(422, \"Unprocessable Content\")\n            print(<><p>Email is required.</p></>)\n            return\n        }\n\n        var session := session_start(\"account\")\n        session = session_set(\"email\", email)\n        response_cookie(\"has_account\", \"1\")\n        redirect(\"/account\")\n        return\n    }\n\n    print(<>\n        <form method=\"post\">\n            <label>Email <input name=\"email\" type=\"email\" /></label>\n            <button>Save</button>\n        </form>\n    </>)\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var files := request.files\n    print(\"files=\", length(files), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "render", caption: "", body: "function RENDER(request : dval) {\n    var session := session_start(\"account\")\n    session = session_set(\"theme\", \"dark\")\n    print(string(session.values.theme, \"\"), \"\\n\")\n}\n"}, {entry: "cli", caption: "", body: "function CLI(request : dval) {\n    print(string(request.body, \"\"))\n}\n"}, {entry: "ws", caption: "", body: "function WS(request : dval) {\n    ws_send(string(request.body, \"\"), false)\n}\n"}])
        return page
    }
    return dval({source_page: "", kind: "", slug: "", title: "Page not found", label: "Page not found", content: ""})
}
